          "Engage the 6.25% non-white population with specific policies on immigrant economic integration, language access, and anti-discrimination enforcement — not token acknowledgment. In homogeneous affluent suburbs, these communities can swing if they perceive invisibility or hostility; concrete attention signals inclusion without abandoning the white homeowner base.",
          "Speak with specificity about education funding: the 29.8% college-educated rate and high homeownership rate describe parents evaluating on school quality and teacher retention. Vague education rhetoric will not persuade this constituency; detailed positions on funding mechanisms and measurable improvement are expected.",
          "Avoid culture-war positioning in favor of demonstrating local governance results — infrastructure maintenance, fiscal accountability, and service delivery. Affluent suburban voters may support conservative values, but they evaluate legislators first on whether government is competent and delivering for their households."
