          { name: "Culture-War Positioning as a Primary Governance Message", tag: "avoid", why: "A district this affluent, educated, and professionally oriented evaluates on economic competence and service delivery, not cultural grievance or ideological performance. Monks' strength is grounded in a 67-33 alignment based on fiscal conservatism and education quality, not partisan culture-war positioning. Overemphasis on social issues at the expense of concrete constituent service or property-tax management risks activating the 33% opposition among college-educated, professional voters who may tolerate conservative governance but reject culture-war rhetoric as a substitute for competence." },
          { name: "Taking the 67-33 Margin as Permanent and Neglecting Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "A 67-33 result is a commanding margin, but in a district with 36.3-year-old professionals, college-educated voters, and $105,501 median income, the constituency expects active, responsive governance and visible district presence—not the quiet confidence of a landslide. Complacency or inattention to constituent service, accessibility, or local issues creates an opening for a credible challenger who can position themselves as more responsive. The 33% opposition is substantial enough that mobilization around specific governance failures or constituent neglect could meaningfully close the margin in a presidential cycle or a race where Monks appears inattentive." }
