          "Lead with working-class homeowner economic security: property tax fairness, rural healthcare viability, agricultural water rights, and cost-of-living stability are the material concerns of a $74,962-median-income, 20.4%-college-educated, 80%-homeowner constituency that generated this 82% result — and they are also the concerns that resonate across both white and Latino working-class families.",
          "Consolidate among non-college-educated voters: with only 20.4% college attainment, this is a district where the incumbent's appeal to working-class material interests rather than credentialed professional concerns is the strategic anchor — doubling down on this positioning reinforces the coalition that produced 82%.",
          "Do not assume the 82% insulates against organized Latino voter mobilization: a credible challenger who mobilizes even modest portions of the unaccounted-for demographic gap and the full Latino-identified 17% around specific economic or immigrant-family concerns could shift 5-8 percentage points in a competitive cycle — treat Latino community engagement as a governance imperative, not an electoral luxury."
