          { name: "Working-Class Homeowner Economic Stability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $73,566 median income with 75.3% homeownership in a district with 24.8% college attainment, this constituency is economically stable but not wealthy — people whose financial security depends on property values holding, local employment remaining viable, and fixed costs (property tax, insurance, utilities) not rising faster than incomes. In an 86% Republican district, the incumbent's ability to retain that margin depends on demonstrating that state policy is protecting these voters' economic foundation. Any candidate who engages homeowner property tax fairness, local business viability, and healthcare cost management with specificity is addressing the material concerns that could persuade the 14% opposition or consolidate the incumbent's existing support." },
          { name: "Local Incumbent Performance & Constituent Service", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 86% result is not explained by demographics alone — an 85.89% white, homeowner-majority, working-class district should produce roughly 65-75% Republican support at baseline, not 86%. The additional 11-21 points of margin reflects the current representative's personal effectiveness, constituent service reputation, or local credibility. For the incumbent, maintaining this margin requires continuous demonstration of constituent responsiveness and local problem-solving. For a challenger, the only path to viability is either recruiting a candidate with equal or greater local credibility and roots, or identifying a specific constituent service failure or governance blind spot that the incumbent has created." },
          { name: "Understanding the 14% Opposition", tag: "careful", why: "In a district this red, 14% opposition is not a demographic baseline — it represents either persuadable voters with specific local concerns, reliable Democratic voters with structural partisan identity, or some combination of both. The incumbent's stability depends on ensuring this 14% does not grow; a challenger's viability depends on converting some portion of it into active support by either addressing homeowners' specific economic frustrations or recruiting a candidate with sufficient local credibility to persuade swing voters that an alternative is viable. The critical strategic question is whether the 14% is fixed or moveable, and understanding that distinction requires direct constituent listening rather than assumption." },
          { name: "Treating 86% as a Durable Structural Result", tag: "avoid", why: "An 86% margin in a district with this demographic profile is not structurally inevitable — it reflects current incumbent performance, constituent satisfaction, and opposition weakness rather than demographic destiny. Any candidate (incumbent or challenger) who assumes this margin will hold or fall based on partisan trends rather than local performance is misreading the actual strategic terrain. In a district this lopsided, everything depends on local factors: incumbent responsiveness, constituent service delivery, and whether a credible alternative exists." },
          { name: "Assuming Opposition Party Irrelevance", tag: "avoid", why: "The 14% Democratic vote in an 86% Republican district suggests either a reliable Democratic base that will turn out regardless of candidate quality, or persuadable voters whose opposition reflects specific frustration with the incumbent. Either way, assuming the opposition is structurally irrelevant is a mistake. A well-recruited Democratic challenger with local roots and authentic credibility could potentially move the margin significantly by activating the existing 14% and persuading persuadable homeowners that an alternative is viable. For the incumbent, complacency is the primary risk; for the opposition, credible recruitment and local legitimacy are the only paths to viability." }
