          { name: "College-Educated Voter Engagement & Policy Substance", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 28.1% college attainment rate is a meaningful share of the electorate that tends to evaluate candidates on competence, policy detail, and whether they have delivered measurable results rather than on partisan identity alone. A challenger (or incumbent opponent) who invests in substance-based communication—detailed education policy, healthcare reform specifics, evidence-based economic development—performs better with this constituency than opponents who rely on partisan messaging. This is a group within the district that has not been fully tapped for persuasion or mobilization." },
          { name: "AIAN Community Engagement & Tribal Governance", tag: "careful", why: "The 5.05% demographic gap almost certainly represents American Indian / Alaska Native residents, roughly 1,000-1,200 people—enough to swing a competitive race if mobilized. Tribal governments, Indian Health Service healthcare access, and Native American economic development are legitimate governance issues in any district with a meaningful AIAN population, and a candidate who engages this community with genuine respect for tribal sovereignty builds credibility while also potentially shifting marginal voters. The incumbent's record of engagement (or lack thereof) with the AIAN community is a specific vulnerability if a challenger identifies it." },
          { name: "Treating 63% as an Unassailable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 63% result in a district with a young median age, high renter rate, and a 5% unaccounted-for AIAN population is a majority but not a landslide and certainly not evidence of deep partisan consolidation. Any incumbent who campaigns as though 63% is a safe floor rather than a contingent coalition is making a strategic error; the 37% opposition in a younger, renter-heavy district may grow if renters face housing shocks or if the AIAN community organizes." },
          { name: "Overlooking the Renter Constituency as a Distinct Voting Bloc", tag: "avoid", why: "The 39.7% renter rate is notably high for an Idaho legislative district and signals a population with different economic concerns than homeowners: no equity accumulation, vulnerability to rent increases, limited access to credit, and dependence on tenant protections and local housing policy. Any candidate who addresses housing and economic security only through homeowner-focused messaging (property tax cuts, land-use deregulation) is leaving 40% of the constituency without a reason to vote for them. Renters who feel their housing needs are invisible are precisely the swing voters a challenger can activate." }
