          "In a supermajority district where partisan competition is functionally absent, the real electoral vulnerability emerges from within-party dynamics and the specific expectations of an affluent, educated, aging constituency. A $94,080 median income and 43.4% college attainment mean this district contains significant numbers of professionals, business owners, and retirees whose governance expectations are concrete and specific: tax policy that protects asset values and business viability, regulation that is predictable rather than punitive, education funding that sustains school quality (a primary driver of property values), healthcare access as median age approaches 45, and local infrastructure maintenance that reflects the district's prosperity. A representative who treats this seat as safely Republican and drifts toward ideological positioning or partisan theater without attending to these material constituent concerns risks the slow erosion of the 85% into the 75-80% range — not enough to lose the seat, but enough to signal that a primary challenger or a more locally-focused alternative might better serve the district's actual needs. The path to holding this supermajority is not partisan mobilization; it is demonstrable competence on the specific issues that matter to affluent, educated, aging homeowners."
